WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.000
Killer robots, Alien invaders, and David&nbsp;
Bowie — yes, the 1970s had it all. Keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.840
watching for the best sci-fi movies&nbsp;
of this weird and wonderful decade.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.340
Today, Westworld is better known as an&nbsp;
acclaimed HBO series — but the show was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.620
actually spawned from a once-forgotten&nbsp;
1973 film that mixed gunslinging action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.300
with sci-fi brilliance. Written by&nbsp;
Jurassic Park author Michael Crichton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.680
Westworld was his directorial debut and&nbsp;
just his second original screenplay.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.280
Westworld is a futuristic&nbsp;
adventure starring Yul Brynner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.600
in a deliberate homage to his role in The&nbsp;
Magnificent Seven. Ten years into the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.160
the Delos Corporation has created a series&nbsp;
of theme parks for the wealthy. One park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.920
aptly known as Westworld, is an extravagant&nbsp;
recreation of the Wild West. Adult guests can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.300
indulge in any desire they wish — but when&nbsp;
a series of cascading program errors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.100
system malfunctions spread throughout the parks,&nbsp;
Westworld's androids develop minds of their own.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.960
"I'm shot."

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:58.620
"What?"

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:00.720
"I'm shot!"

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.380
One of several films to warn of the coming dangers&nbsp;
of artificial intelligence and robot technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.860
Westworld was praised by Variety&nbsp;
for its "chilling topicality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.100
and superbly intelligent&nbsp;
serio-comic story values."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.132
It was followed by a lesser sequel, Futureworld,&nbsp;
and a single-season TV spin-off, Beyond Westworld.

00:01:23.597 --> 00:01:28.080
Not every great '70s science fiction&nbsp;
film was a blockbuster. Released in 1970,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.520
Colossus: The Forbin Project is an&nbsp;
underrated classic that crosses genres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.320
being equal parts frightening science fiction&nbsp;
parable and riveting political thriller. Based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.880
on a novel by Dennis Feltham Jones, the movie&nbsp;
warns of the dangers of artificial intelligence.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:47.460
In a grim near-future on the brink of war, Dr.&nbsp;
Charles Forbin has designed a highly intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.020
supercomputer called Colossus, which is intended&nbsp;
to control the nuclear arsenal of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.000
States and its allies. Soon, however, Forbin&nbsp;
realizes that the Soviet Union has their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.400
device, and he and his colleagues watch helplessly&nbsp;
as the two AI systems begin to work together.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.980
A gripping science fiction thriller, Colossus:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.420
The Forbin Project dispenses with action&nbsp;
and adventure, instead opting for a slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.880
deliberately-paced narrative set mostly&nbsp;
in boardrooms and control rooms. What the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.472
movie lacks in action, however, it&nbsp;
more than makes up for in suspense.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:24.360
In the late 1960s, an aspiring young director&nbsp;
named George Lucas crafted a 15-minute science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.040
fiction short about a totalitarian state&nbsp;
that demands a total lack of emotion from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:36.720
its citizens. It was titled Electronic Labyrinth:&nbsp;
THX 1138 4EB. The movie became the basis for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.920
of the first projects distributed by Warner&nbsp;
Bros. under the American Zoetrope banner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.880
a production house started by Francis&nbsp;
Ford Coppola to nurture creative young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.540
talent and independent filmmaking. The full-length&nbsp;
feature, THX 1138, was Lucas' feature film debut.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.760
THX 1138 is set in an underground&nbsp;
world devoid of love, passion, hate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.380
or anger, as free will and thought are&nbsp;
squashed by the state. When one man and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.620
one woman stop taking the medication&nbsp;
that suppresses feelings, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.180
they are confronted by the true horror of their&nbsp;
world — and fall in love in the process. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.200
experience emotions for the first time… and&nbsp;
immediately become fugitives from the law.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.980
A glimpse into a harrowing future, THX 1138 gained&nbsp;
more attention after the release of Lucas' Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.540
Wars, and has since become a revered classic&nbsp;
for its exploration of the nature of free will.

00:03:25.105 --> 00:03:28.691
"What do you do? For a living, I mean?"

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.240
"Oh, just visiting."

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.740
Many cult classics can stake a claim&nbsp;
among the best sci-fi films of the 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.960
but are none quite as unusual and offbeat&nbsp;
as The Man Who Fell to Earth. Most notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.400
perhaps for its lead performance by David Bowie,&nbsp;
the film was barely given a theatrical release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.320
reportedly because Paramount executives were&nbsp;
so perplexed by it. Over the years, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.340
The Man Who Fell to Earth has garnered&nbsp;
a passionate fan following, not just for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.020
Bowie's charming and affecting performance, but&nbsp;
also for its eerie tone and compelling story.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.700
Bowie stars as an alien who hails from a&nbsp;
world on which an environmental crisis has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.880
left the planet dry and barren.&nbsp;
Coming to Earth seeking water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:11.040
he takes on a human identity and sets himself&nbsp;
up as the CEO of a major technology firm in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.180
order to save his homeworld. Soon, though,&nbsp;
he becomes a wealthy tycoon and slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.020
falls prey to Earth's many temptations —&nbsp;
including the love of a beautiful woman.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:24.000
Today, The Man Who Fell to Earth is&nbsp;
considered a bona fide sci-fi gem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.258
and its influence lives on in a surprising&nbsp;
limited series remake that was launched in 2022.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.800
It's hard to remake a classic movie and&nbsp;
do it justice, but in the 1978 Invasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.940
of the Body Snatchers did that and then&nbsp;
some, retelling the same story as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:43.500
1956 film of the same name. Sometimes&nbsp;
cited as one of the best remakes ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.600
the film brought the story of a sinister alien&nbsp;
invasion into the 1970s, mixing it with the tone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.920
of the decade's best political thrillers and&nbsp;
loading it with relevant social commentary.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:58.320
In this frightening reboot of the original movie,&nbsp;
we meet San Francisco-based scientific researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.360
Elizabeth Driscoll, who is disturbed when her&nbsp;
boyfriend begins acting strangely. It soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.080
becomes clear that a strange alien force&nbsp;
is creating duplicates of human beings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.160
forcing Driscoll and her allies to&nbsp;
flee from a city of doppelgangers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:16.020
A stunning, suspense-filled alien invasion&nbsp;
story with few equals, Invasion of the Body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.077
Snatchers also happens to boast one of the&nbsp;
bleakest endings in sci-fi movie history.

00:05:21.077 --> 00:05:25.500
The subgenre of post-apocalyptic&nbsp;
sci-fi gained steam in the 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.420
and it arguably reached its zenith with the&nbsp;
ultra-low budget Australian film Mad Max.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:35.940
George Miller's ability to carefully craft a high&nbsp;
quality futuristic action movie on a shoestring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.500
budget shook the filmmaking world, with a cottage&nbsp;
industry of wannabes springing up in its wake.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:45.660
In Mad Max, most of society has all but&nbsp;
fallen apart due to global oil shortages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.980
As survivors try to eke out a meager&nbsp;
living, local warlords and gangs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.120
ruthless bikers attempt to take advantage of&nbsp;
the chaos — but one of the last remnants of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.500
organized law enforcement, a cop named&nbsp;
Max Rockatansky, is out to stop them.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.780
Still one of the best movies of its genre, Mad&nbsp;
Max was a sensation in its native Australia thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.720
to its gear-grinding action and over-the-top&nbsp;
violence. It found just as much success Stateside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.340
turning Mel Gibson into a star and kickstarting&nbsp;
George Miller's directorial career. Eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.660
followed by a pair of sequels, Mad Max&nbsp;
was later rebooted in 2015 with Mad Max:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:24.150
Fury Road, a critically acclaimed blockbuster&nbsp;
that reignited the franchise for the 21st century.

00:06:24.475 --> 00:06:27.238
"Oh, what a day! What a lovely day!"

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.820
The man behind some of the&nbsp;
best movies of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.840
Stanley Kubrick also directed one of&nbsp;
the greatest dystopian sci-fi classics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.820
A Clockwork Orange. Based on the&nbsp;
celebrated 1962 novel by Anthony Burgess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:43.680
the movie stars Malcolm McDowell and a broad&nbsp;
ensemble cast of lesser-known actors in a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.180
about a dismal future that mixes unsettling&nbsp;
surrealism with sharp social commentary.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.880
A film that often borders on the avant-garde, A&nbsp;
Clockwork Orange revolves around Alex DeLarge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.620
an eccentric street punk who leads an&nbsp;
ultra-violent gang of thugs as they steal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.320
assault, and murder innocent people. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.300
Alex is apprehended by the authorities, who force&nbsp;
him to undergo a brutal form of brainwashing.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.140
A film that's cinematically breathtaking&nbsp;
and disturbing in equal measure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:15.386
A Clockwork Orange is today considered to be&nbsp;
one of the truly iconic films of the 1970s.

00:07:15.836 --> 00:07:20.340
Young director Steven Spielberg had done plenty&nbsp;
of work for television in the early 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.840
and his transition to feature films yielded&nbsp;
results almost immediately. In his second film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.620
Jaws, he produced one of the biggest hits&nbsp;
of the decade and popularized the term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.580
"blockbuster." To follow it up, he turned to&nbsp;
science fiction, with a poignant story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:38.460
mixed family drama and an outer space mystery: the&nbsp;
1977 classic Close Encounters of the Third Kind.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.020
While Star Wars blew audiences away with&nbsp;
its fast-moving inspace opera action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.200
Spielberg went a different route,&nbsp;
opting for an emotional drama told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.880
through a story of alien visitation.&nbsp;
Here, family man Roy Neary is contacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.900
by an extraterrestrial intelligence and&nbsp;
becomes a conduit for communication when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:58.440
they arrive on Earth. Neary's personal&nbsp;
journey sits at the heart of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:02.760
as he struggles to understand what is happening&nbsp;
while his family life deteriorates around him.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.680
A visually captivating experience that&nbsp;
fuses mystery, terror, wonder, and awe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.280
Close Encounters of the Third Kind&nbsp;
was nominated for nine Academy Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:15.240
including Best Director for Spielberg. While&nbsp;
it may not have been the biggest sci-fi film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.260
at the box office in 1977, it&nbsp;
was probably the most moving.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.080
Based on a novel by Polish author Stanislaw&nbsp;
Lem, the landmark film Solaris may be Russia's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.820
greatest contribution to the sci-fi genre. While&nbsp;
many American audiences still haven't given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:34.380
the film its proper due, critics and sci-fi&nbsp;
buffs have rightly hailed it as a milestone.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.720
Released in 1972, the film centers on Kris Kelvin,&nbsp;
a psychologist who is sent to a space station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:44.340
orbiting the planet Solaris. On a mission&nbsp;
to assess the mental condition of its crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:49.140
following a series of unexplainable emotional&nbsp;
breakdowns, Kris soon begins to suffer himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:54.060
due to an unknown phenomenon that has struck the&nbsp;
station. As he learns more about the aliens and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:59.460
Solaris itself, Klein's experience also helps him&nbsp;
deal with trauma that has haunted him for years.

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:03.960
A spellbinding psychological drama,&nbsp;
Solaris has always been highly acclaimed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.580
but long underrated and underseen by&nbsp;
wider audiences. That perhaps helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:13.440
explain why director Steven Soderbergh and&nbsp;
star George Clooney remade the film in 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.678
in an effort to shine a light&nbsp;
on an under-appreciated classic.

00:09:16.895 --> 00:09:19.800
"You've just taken your first&nbsp;
step into a larger world."

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.240
George Lucas' sci-fi phenomenon Star Wars&nbsp;
was enough of a cultural touchstone that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.440
it deserves to be counted among the best&nbsp;
sci-fi films of the decade for its legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.880
alone. Star Wars debuted to massive crowds&nbsp;
and lines that wrapped around city blocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.580
while children skipped school&nbsp;
and adults took off work to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:39.360
see it. Boasting groundbreaking special&nbsp;
effects, the movie is a modern-day fairy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.620
tale about a young group of heroes who band&nbsp;
together to defeat an evil galactic empire.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:48.060
With its unprecedented ticket sales&nbsp;
and mountainous merchandising blitz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:52.920
Star Wars changed the landscape of movies forever.&nbsp;
Every studio wanted a piece of the pie, and while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:58.440
sequels were nothing new in Tinseltown, the era of&nbsp;
blockbuster franchises and toy tie-ins truly began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.780
in 1977. And it wasn't just the special effects,&nbsp;
the lunch boxes, and the box office bucks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:08.040
either: Star Wars was also a well-crafted&nbsp;
story with a heartfelt emotional core.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.600
Despite being a kid-friendly, pulp comic&nbsp;
book space story a la Flash Gordon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:17.820
critics were bowled over, showering the film&nbsp;
with gushing praise. Noted critic Roger Ebert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.580
who wasn't typically impressed&nbsp;
by flimsy blockbuster fare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:25.860
happily awarded it four stars and remarked on&nbsp;
its awe-inspiring nature. No matter how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.840
look at it, when it comes to great '70s sci-fi&nbsp;
adventures, Star Wars is unquestionably king.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:36.060
The 1970s were filled with great science&nbsp;
fiction movies that ran the gamut from cerebral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:40.980
thought-provoking message films to&nbsp;
breezy space adventures. But in 1979,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:46.320
an iconic film from sophomore director Ridley&nbsp;
Scott did something truly staggering. A grounded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:51.000
realistic outer space chiller that was unlike&nbsp;
anything audiences had experienced before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.960
Alien delivered a story that expertly&nbsp;
blended horror and science fiction.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.420
Screenwriter Dan O'Bannon created a world that&nbsp;
mixed the lived-in grittiness of Star Wars with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.740
a cast of ordinary men and women, rather than&nbsp;
larger-than-life heroes and colorful creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.020
These were not intrepid space adventurers,&nbsp;
but blue-collar workers, long-haul space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:14.700
truckers dealing with an extraordinary problem:&nbsp;
a deadly alien stowaway that threatens to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:19.140
every last one of them. The terrifying&nbsp;
creature, later known as a xenomorph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.980
would instantly become one of Hollywood's&nbsp;
all-time greatest movie monsters.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:29.040
Sigourney Weaver led the film as Ellen Ripley,&nbsp;
an unlikely woman protagonist in an era where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.600
dashing male heroes dominated the screen. Her&nbsp;
impressive performance inspired a generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:39.660
as her indomitable presence made Ripley a force&nbsp;
to be reckoned with. Eschewing fast-paced space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:43.980
action for slowly-building tension dripping&nbsp;
with atmosphere, the film's stylish direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.440
gripping suspense, and sheer terror elevates&nbsp;
Alien to the greatest sci-fi movie of the 1970s.

